/-!
# The fpqc topology is finer than the other standard topologies

The fpqc topology on schemes is finer than the étale topology, and an fpqc sheaf on the
category of schemes over a base `S` restricts to a sheaf along the canonical inclusion of a
chosen big or small Zariski site, a chosen big or big affine étale site, a chosen small affine
étale site, a chosen big or big affine fppf site, or a chosen big or big affine smooth or
syntomic site of `S`.

## Main results

* The `AlgebraicGeometry.isSheaf_*_of_isSheaf_fpqc` family: an fpqc sheaf on schemes over `S`
  restricts to a sheaf on the chosen big and small Zariski sites, on the chosen big, big affine
  and small affine étale sites, on the chosen big and big affine fppf sites, and on the chosen
  big and big affine smooth and syntomic sites of `S`.

## Implementation notes

The étale and fpqc topologies are compared on the common category of all schemes, where each
is the Grothendieck topology generated by the precoverage of jointly surjective families with
the corresponding property of morphisms; the comparison then follows from the corresponding
comparison of precoverages. The chosen-site statements use the site topologies with the
presheaf restricted along the canonical inclusion functor.

Each chosen-site statement is obtained by showing that the canonical inclusion into the
schemes over `S` is continuous for the fpqc topology. That reduces to the single fact that
the image in schemes of a chosen covering family is an fpqc covering family, which is where
the topology-specific input enters. The inclusions create pullbacks because they are fully
faithful and preserve them; for the affine variants this uses that a pullback of affine
schemes over an affine scheme is affine, and for the smooth and syntomic cutoff categories
that the size bound is stable under pullback.

For the big Zariski site over `S` the fibre products are those of a slice of the cutoff
category, and for the small Zariski site they are
`AlgebraicGeometry.hasPullbacks_smallZariski` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.preservesPullbacks_smallZariskiInclusion`, taken from
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Topologies.BigSites.ZariskiLimits` rather than reproved here.
The two affine Zariski sites
are absent: their covering families are the standard ones, so their inclusions do not send
every covering family of the ambient site to a covering family.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 03O4](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03O4)
-/

@[expose] public section

